  "title": "CHAPTER 14: ECONOMIC PLANNING & FIVE-YEAR PLANS IN INDIA",
  "order": 84,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "vene2ay",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PART A: THE BASIC ECONOMIC PROBLEM AND NEED FOR PLANNING"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "India adopted economic planning post-independence to combat poverty, low growth, and structural inadequacies in market forces.",
        "The planning era spanned 12 Five-Year Plans, starting from the First Plan (1951–56) through the Twelfth Plan (2012–17).",
        "Key disruptions in five-year cycles included the Plan Holidays (1966–69) and the Rolling Plan (1978–80).",
        "In 2015, the government transitioned from the centralized Planning Commission to NITI Aayog to serve as a policy think tank."
      ],
      "id": "jrt2rn2"
    },
